{"page_id": "olmo_long_tiny_text-11_pg57_pg1-p1", "markdown": "# LOP\n\n529\n\nLOR\n\n(4) The privilege of turning out cattle on com-\n\nAs a lopinebbe fileth fome and gnattis, Taken and suffren gret files go.\n\nLOOT.\n\nA thin oblong square board fixed to a\n\nOccleve, MS. Soc. Antiq, 134, f. 267.\n\nstaff or handle, used in boiling brine to re-\n\nLOQUINTUE.\n\nEloquent, Weber.\n\nmove the scum.\n\nStaff.\n\nLORD. (1) A title of honour given to monks\n\nLOOTH.\n\nThe same as Loo, q. v.\n\nand persons of superior rank. (4.-S.)\n\nLOOVER. An opening at the top of a dove-cote\n\n2) Lord have mercy upon us was formerly the\n\nNorth.\n\nSee Lover (2).\n\ninscription\n\non\n\nhouses infected with the\n\nLOOVEYD.\n\nPraised.\n\nRitson.\n\nplague.\n\nLord have mercy upon me, a diseasc\n\nLOOVEYNG.\n\nPraise; honour.\n\nthus mentioned in the Nomenclator, \"the\n\nThat was a feyre tokenynge\n\nIlliake passion, or a paine and wringing in the\n\nOf pees and of looreyng.\n\nsmall guts, which the homelier sort of phisi-\n\nLOOZE Laughed.\n\nMS. Cantab. Ff. li. 38, f. 162.\n\ncians doc call, Lorde have mercy upon me.\"\n\nA pig-stye.\n\nL003.\n\nWest.\n\nLORDEYN.\n\nSee Fever-Laurden.\n\n«i The lurgy -\n\n\" At hym ful fast thei loos,\"\n\nfever, idleness,\" Craven Glossary, p. 304.\n\nMS. Cantat. Ff. t.48, 1. 06.\n\n1 trow he was infecte certeyn\n\nLOP. (1) A flea. North.\n\n（A.-S.）\n\nWith the faitour, or the fever lordeyn,\n\nYs joy y-now so ye your lyggys streyne,\n\nMS. Rawl. C. 86, xv. Cent.\n\nYe lade longe-sydyde as a loppe.\n\nLORD-FEST.\n\nMS. Fairfas 16.\n\nExcessively lordly. (4.-S.)\n\n2) To lollop or lounge about.\n\nKent.\n\nLORDINGS, Sirs; masters. (4.-S.)\n\nIt is often\n\n(3) To hang loosely; to hang down, or droop.\n\nused by later writers in contempt.\n\nLORD-OF-MISRULE.\n\nThe person who pre-\n\nVar. dial\n\nsided over the Christmas revels, by no means\n\n4) The faggot wood of a tree.\n\nLOPE.\n\nLeapt.\n\nAlso, to leap.\n\nan ummportant personage in the olden times.\n\nIt seems to be\n\na subst. in the second example.\n\nHe began his rule on All-hallow eve and con-\n\nAs sone as the chylde had spoke,\n\ntinued it till Candlemas day. See a list of exences, dated in 1552, in Kempe's Losele\n\nThe fende ynto hym was lope.\n\nNS. Harl、 1701, f.40.\n\nManuscripts, pp. 44-54.\n\nor further informa\n\ntion on the subject, see Brand, i. 272; Arch.\n\nTyme goth fast, it is full lyght of lope,\n\nxviii, 313-335; Hawkins' Engl. Dram. iii. 156;\n\nAnd in abydyng men seyn ther lyghte hope.\n\nMS. Rawl. Poet. 118.\n\nStrutt, ii. 200; Lilly's Sixe Court Comedies,\n\nLOP-EARED. Having long pendulous ears like a hound. Var. dial.\n\nLOUDS AND. LADIES.\n\nSee Bulls-and-Cows.\n\nLOPEN.\n\nLeapt.\n\nLORDSHIP.\n\nSee the Sevyn Sages, 739.\n\nSupreme power. (4.-S.)\n\nLORD-SIZE\n\nWhan thy mouthe with shryfte ys opun\n\nThe judge at the assizes.\n\nDeth and synne are bothe oute lopan.\n\nLORD'S-ROOM.\n\nThe stage-box in a theatre\n\nMS. Haxl. 1701, f. 79.\n\nwas formerly so called. Jonson.\n\nThe portar set the yatys opon,\n\nLORDSWYK.\n\nA traitor.\n\nRitson.\n\nAnd with that Befyse ys owt lopon.\n\nLORE. (1) Knowledge; doctrine ; advice. (4.-S.)\n\nMS. Cantab. Ff. II. 33, f. 108.\n\nAnoon was al that feire gederynge\n\n(2) Lost.\n\nStill in use in Somerset.\n\nLopen undir oure lordes wynge.\n\nThe kyng seld, Take me thy tayle,\n\nCureor Mundi, MS. Coll, Trin, Cantab, f. 111.\n\nFor my hors I wolde not the fayle,\n\nSythen he ys lopen on hys stede,\n\nA peny that thou fore.\n\nMS. Cantab. FL. T. 40, C. 51.\n\nHe with hym Harrawde dud lede.\n\nMS. Cantab. Fl. iI. 38, f. 154.\n\nLOREFADYR. A teacher. Loremastir, Dial.\n\nCreat. Moral. p. 243.\n\nLOPE-STAFF. A leaping-staff.\n\n« A lope-staffe\n\nOf al men they do most evyl,\n\nwherewith men leape ditches,\" Cotgrave.\n\nLOPIRD. Coagulated. Still in use. See Forby,\n\nHere lorefadyr ys the devyl. MS. Harl. 1701, f 24,\n\nLOREINE.\n\nA rein.\n\nSee Lannfal, 888.\n\nBrockett, Grose, Kennett, &c.\n\nHys treine lemyd alle with pride,\n\nThare he fande none other fode,\n\nStede and armure alle was blake.\n\nBot wlatesome glete and lopird blode\n\nMS. Harl.9252, f.104.\n\nMS. Lincoln A. 1. 17, f. 276.\n\nLOREL.\n\nA bad worthless fellow.\n\n（4.-N.）\n\nThare dwelled a man in a myrke donjowne,\n\nLorels den, Holinshed, Chron. Ireland, p. 93.\n\nAnd In a fowle stede of corrupeyowne,\n\nCocke Lorel was formerly a generic title for\n\nWhare he had no fode,\n\na very great rascal.\n\nBot wlatsome glette and lopyrd blode.\n\n\" Lasy lorrels,\" Harman,\n\n1567.\n\nLOP-LOACH.\n\nHampole, MS. Bowes, p. 25.\n\nThe leech used by surgeons for\n\nLOREMAR. A bit-maker. Palsgrave. \" Lori.\n\ndrawing blood.\n\nNorth.\n\nmers or bit-makers,\" Harrison, p. 97.\n\nLOPLOLLY• Lame.\n\nLORENGE.\n\nA lazy fellow. West.\n\nIron.（4.-N.）\n\nLOPPING.\n\nDorset.\n\nLORER.\n\nThe laurel-tree.\n\nChaucer.\n\nThis Daphne into a lorer tre\n\nLOP-SIDED. One-sided. Var. dial.\n\nLOP-START.\n\nThe stoat.\n\nWas turnid, whiche is ever grene.\n\nEast.\n\nIt is men-\n\nGower, MS. Soc. Antiq. 134, f. 95.\n\ntioned in Harrison's England, p. 230.\n\nAnd plaunted trees that were to preise,\n\nLOPUSTER.\n\nA lobster.\n\nOf cldre, palme, and of lorers,\n\nLOPWEBBE.\n\n11.\n\nA spider's web. (4.-S.)\n\nCursor Mundi, MS. Coll. Trin. Cantab. f. 52,\n\n34", "width": 987, "height": 1612, "blocks": [{"kind": "heading", "text": "LOP", "bbox": {"x": 211.49999986571427, "y": 73.88333325497213, "width": 60.42857142857144, "height": 23.50833333333343}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "529", "bbox": {"x": 453.2142855335165, "y": 73.88333323000002, "width": 47, "height": 26.86666666666657}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "LOR", "bbox": {"x": 678.142856964509, "y": 77.24166660369795, "width": 57.07142857142853, "height": 20.149999999999928}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "(4) The privilege of turning out cattle on com-", "bbox": {"x": 36.92857126228307, "y": 117.54166664783489, "width": 429.7142857142857, "height": 40.3000000000